<p class="para lt-chem-349754" id="av_1.0-ch16_s02_s02_p05">Weak bases react with water to produce the hydroxide ion, as shown in the following general equation, where B is the parent base and BH<sup>+</sup> is its conjugate acid:</p>

<p class="lt-chem-349754">
</p><p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-349754">\[B_{(aq)}+H_2O_{(l)} \rightleftharpoons BH^+_{(aq)}+OH^&minus;_{(aq)} \tag{16.2.4}\]</p>


<p class="para block lt-chem-349754" id="av_1.0-ch16_s02_s02_p06">The equilibrium constant for this reaction is the base ionization constant (<em>K</em><sub>b</sub>)<span class="glossdef">An equilibrium constant for the reaction of a weak base (B) with water, <span class="inlineequation">B(aq)</span> + <span class="inlineequation">H<sub>2</sub>O(l)</span> &#8652; <span class="inlineequation">BH<sup>+</sup>(aq)</span> + <span class="inlineequation">OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq),</span> in which the concentration of water is treated as a constant: <span class="inlineequation">Kb</span> = <span class="inlineequation">[BH<sup>+</sup>][OH<sup>-</sup>]/[B]</span></span>, also called the <em>base dissociation constant</em>:</p>

<p class="lt-chem-349754">
</p><p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-349754">\[K_b=K[H_2O]=[BH^+][OH^&minus;][B] \tag{16.2.5}\]</p>


<p class="para lt-chem-349754" id="av_1.0-ch16_s02_s02_p07">Once again, the concentration of water is constant, so it does not appear in the equilibrium constant expression; instead, it is included in the <em>K</em><sub>b</sub>. The larger the <em>K</em><sub>b</sub>, the stronger the base and the higher the OH<sup>&minus;</sup> concentration at equilibrium. The values of <em>K</em><sub>b</sub> for a number of common weak bases are given in <a href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Courses/Howard_University/General_Chemistry%3A_An_Atoms_First_Approach/Unit_6%3A_Kinetics_and_Equilibria/Chapter_16%3A_Aqueous_Acid-Base_Equilibria/Chapter_16.2%3A_A_Qualitative_Description_of__Acid-Base_Equilibria#av_1.0-ch16_s02_s02_t02" rel="internal">Table 16.2.2</a></p>

